hi alfonso,

i must say: wow, i find really impressive. i'm very thankful to see the 
rkwarddev package being used so productively.

Am Donnerstag, 12. November 2026, 18:05:43 Mitteleuropäische Normalzeit 
schrieb DES Alfonso Cano Robles:
> A Featured Community Repository / List: If the RKWard package manager
> (Manage R packages and plugins) could natively feature or pre-configure
> external repository URLs like my R-Universe
> (https://alfcano.r-universe.dev), users would see rk.universe (and its 70+
> modules) directly under "New Packages" without having to manually type out
> custom repos parameters.

aren't we already doing this with https://files.kde.org/rkward/R (see 
"settings" -> "configure RKWard..." -> "add-ons" -> "R-packages", under 
"additional package repositories"; ). adding the rk.universe repository seem 
like a good move. that would be the quickest way to make the plugins more 
visible for users.

we could perhaps make both https://files.kde.org/rkward/R and https://
alfcano.r-universe.dev checkable additions instead of just writing them into 
the list of custom repos, which would more clearly separate them from actual 
custom entries. unselecting them allows users to reenable them easily should 
they reconsider at a later point. but that's a detail.

> 1. A "Get More Plugins" / Community Section: Having a dedicated subsection
> in the settings or help menu that links to community-driven categories like:

except for the categorization, we're half way there as well. the dashboard 
already offers "Install R and RKWard packages" under "Useful settings". 
currently, this is one link opening the dialog you also get when choosing 
"settings" -> "manage R packages and plugins...". the second tab has the 
checkbox to "show only packages providing RKWard dialogues".

can this dialog be opened showing the second tab with that checkbox checked? 
if so, as an improvement i would suggest to split "Install R and RKWard 
packages" in the dashboard into "[Install R packages](open dialog as before) 
or [RKWard plugins](open second tab checked)". it would then take only one 
click to get to the list of installable plugin packages.

i also like the idea of offering categories, but that would need more planning 
on how to achieve this. one way could be to add new variables to the 
DESCRIPTION of these packages, e.g., "Config/category" and/or "Config/tags". 
the latter is more versatile as you could use multiple tags for a package 
instead of one category. however, RKWard would need a method of parsing and 
displaying this additional information in a helpful way.


viele grüße
m.eik
